ork which are five  all-girls public schools six through  twelfth grade with a hundred percent  graduation rate and I was just blown  away by these schools I am born and  raised in Baltimore  my mom's born and raised in Baltimore  has been an activists there my whole  life for women and girls I grew up with  a domestic violence hotline being run  from my dining room table so I had the  kind of mother that I could suggest  maybe she should replicate  to school like these schools I was  working with in Baltimore and she did  and she recruited her daughter to make  films for her and I did so I met these  young ladies when they were 11 years old  and I started making shorts just to  promote the school and promote the good  work that everyone was doing there and I  knew that the step team had started what  I wasn't familiar with staff it wasn't  something that was a part of my culture  growing up it didn't know about the rich  history coming from Africa I didn't know  about it being a collegiate sport now in  North America but in the 8th grade I was  filming blessing for another project in  New York we were she was actually  interviewing sherrilyn Ifill at the ACLU  and she looked at me and she said you  know the next time you come to school  with cameras you really need to come  film our step team and I said okay  because I was always drawn to blessing  you know she kind of linked her arm with  mine when she was 11 and said you're a  Broadway producer I'm going to be on  Broadway and I believed her and I just  remember walking in and my heart stopped  beating because of every stomp with  every clap with every breath they were  expressing themselves and that's what  happens in a great musical you know in a  great musical a character can't speak  anymore so they sing to express their  fears and their hopes and their dreams  and that's what they were doing and I  knew them well enough at that point to  know that I saw every range of girl on  that team and that there was a 