ed to see them  stamped out we needed to see the feet  come down so all I mean for us Freddie  Gray's death turned the flame up in all  of us and I think made these young gave  these young women and their families the  courage and the strength to really tell  the story  coach gee and the girls when you're  talking about the way in which you can  take Freddie Gray's murder and make it  part of your step what were the  conversations that you had as a team and  as a teacher and what were the things  that you wanted to bring to light not  just through performance but through  rehearsal and talking about his death  that you think were important so coach  she wanted you start and we'll talk to  the girls as well for me what was  important was for them to realize this  is a wonderful magical place but it's a  very small charter school Baltimore  filled with so many people who love them  and believe in them and tell them that  the sky's the limit that's very true  there's so many people who are going to  trip you up in the way of trying to  reach the sky we're going to pull you  back and as young black women you know  we have it twice as hard and people  don't you don't walk into a room and  people  actually be equally as adequate as they  are and that's just a reality so I  wanted them to be realistic about what  is out there in the real world they were  always taught to be self advocates they  were always taught to stand up for  themselves and have confidence and all  of those things but my conversation was  how do you feel about this more  social-emotional how do you feel about  what happened where were you what were  you doing what were you thinking what  was going through your mind when we did  that routine for black lives matters it  was a Baltimore routine and a lot of  also more stuff competition and a lot of  competitions give you guidelines as to  what you can make the theme and the  first thing that came to my mind was it  was almost a year after Freddie gray  died and I was like oh definitely I'm  gonna do black lives matters because  after Freddie gray was murdered somebody  else was murdered and then somebody else  was murdered so these are things that I  wanted them to understand as young black  women when you walk out of these doors  it could be you it could be me it could  be any of you and I think that it was  important for me to change the  conversation about black lives matters  to not being a hateful thing or saying  that other lives don't matter because  that's not what that statement is about  it's about the fact that everyone has  children and no one wants to bury their  child and the reality is if these young  girls going to a charter school that's  college prep are putting their hands up  and saying don't shoot that's exactly  what these young men and women who are  victims of these crimes of these  atrocities that's that's exactly what  happened to them so if you can imagine  them in that place then you can think  like oh this could be my child you have  that empathy empathy to think this could  be my child that's beautifully set  lesson Cory and Caleb what do you think  about what your coach just said about  the ways in which you can use step to  tell a story and come together and  express something that you're feeling  and share it with a bigger audience  I completely agree um let me start off  in sixth grade there was just something  fun to do it looked cool and we really  liked the costumes and makeup and stuff  but over the years it really started to  become so  much more than just entertainment and I  think that's something that's really  important that we appreciate closely for  because she helped us to you know kind  of cross over from just entertaining to  also educating people because there are  people who don't understand where we  come from or who don't understand the  things that we go through the  adversities that we face on a daily  basis whether those be social or  political or academic or with our  families and really step is really just  that platform for us to share our  experiences I agree with Cory Cory  actually a lot of coaches well we've had  a lot of coaches in their focus was  entertainment and no one ever really  taught us how to educate when we step  and mostly we learnt that people listen  to you when you're with a group and  you're loud and you're stomping and  you're clapping and you have fun while  you're doing it and I think that when  you're educating you should have fun  while you're doing it and make sure that  you have a purpose Laura I want to ask a  little bit about how you incorporate a  smiie Annie I'm not I'm going to ask you  one thing about incorporating music into  we're into a story in which music and  rhythm to figure so prominently when  you're working with Raphael Saadiq and  trying to figure out how you're going to  place music in this you obviously pun  intended don't want to step on the  stepping so what do you look for how  does music accentuate what is already an  incredibly emotional story what is the  texture and depth that it can give a  documentary like this well they're  heroes so I think that the first thing  to do is to write music that's heroic  but intimate and that it's a it's a  small story about everyday heroes but of  course it's a huge story so I think that  was the the challenge and and the goal  of it and also just to stay out of the  way yeah which is not my not me really  you know I'm a kind of get in the way  kind of gal but with Amanda I think that  was the discipline here stay out of the  way set up the sort of the theatricality  of it come into play I mean important  montages is the is the pyramid montage  and then of course the song at the end  those are those are the places where  where we were allowed to shine musically  and every place else we're kind of like  don't do too much by the way I just want  to say I've done a lot of panels here I  think it's the first thing I've done  with six women up yeah so and I think  that's amazing because I also think this  is a story about women I think it's a  story about moms and I think we should  give a shout out to all the moms yeah  you guys thank you so much for coming  out

